module bindings.python.oauth_email_claim_username;

// JupyterHub's OAuthenticator resolves a login to a local account by reading one
// claim out of the provider's userinfo response: `username_claim` names it, and a
// subclass declares its default with traitlets' documented dynamic-default hook,
// `@default("username_claim") def _username_claim_default(self)`. Whatever that
// method returns is the account the login lands on.
//
// OpenID Connect Core 5.7 is explicit that the `email` claim is not usable that
// way on its own: the address is mutable, reassignable, and set by whoever
// controls the upstream account, so a provider ships `email_verified` beside it to
// say whether it ever confirmed the address. Google's userinfo spells the same
// attestation `verified_email`, GitLab's a `confirmed_at` timestamp, Bitbucket's
// an `is_confirmed` flag per address. A relying party that names `email` as its
// account identity and never reads any of them lets anyone who can type a victim's
// address into an unconfirmed provider account sign in as that victim
// (CWE-287/CWE-290).
//
// The claim selection is the fact: an authenticator deriving from OAuthenticator
// or from its generic OIDC subclass whose `username_claim` default is the email
// address. The default has to be unconditional -- the method body ends at
// `return "email"` -- because a default that branches on configuration
// (`if self.username_from_email: return "email"; return "preferred_username"`)
// names some other claim for an unconfigured deployment, and the address is then
// the identity only where an operator asked for it.
//
// The neutraliser is any read of an attestation the provider actually supplies,
// anywhere on the authenticator class, because the check belongs in `check_allowed`
// or `check_blocked_users` rather than beside the claim selection. It is spelled
// with the quotes or the leading dot the lookup itself carries, so that the class
// body's prose -- which Python hands to every method's context verbatim, comments
// and docstrings included -- cannot decide the result. Being generous here costs
// recall, not precision: an authenticator that reads the attestation and then falls
// back past it is not reported, and that is the right trade for keeping every
// correctly-checking provider quiet.
binding oauthEmailClaimUsernameUnverified {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.token contains "lang=python"
    and node.context.functionName == "_username_claim_default"
    and containsAny(node.context.classBase, ["OAuthenticator", "GenericOAuthenticator", "LocalGenericOAuthenticator"])
    and node.token endsWith "return\"email\""
    and not containsAny(node.token, ["\"email_verified\"", "'email_verified'", "\"verified_email\"", "'verified_email'", "\"email_confirmed\"", "'email_confirmed'", "\"confirmed_at\"", "'confirmed_at'", "\"is_confirmed\"", "'is_confirmed'", ".email_verified", ".verified_email", ".email_confirmed"])
  emit issue code.UnverifiedEmailClaimIdentity at node
}
